1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,154
If you skipped straight to this lecture hoping to skip the paperwork, here is the

2
00:00:05,154 --> 00:00:08,771
argument again, briefly, because it matters enough to repeat.

3
00:00:08,771 --> 00:00:11,974
A job description is not bureaucracy for its own sake.

4
00:00:11,974 --> 00:00:16,718
It is the only thing standing between "I connected an inbox" and "I connected an

5
00:00:16,718 --> 00:00:21,462
inbox, on purpose, to do one specific job." What actually goes wrong when people

6
00:00:21,462 --> 00:00:23,182
build first and decide later?

7
00:00:23,182 --> 00:00:27,927
The scope gets negotiated mid-build, with the least cautious person in the room,

8
00:00:27,927 --> 00:00:29,943
which is you at your most excited.

9
00:00:29,943 --> 00:00:34,747
The workflow nearly works, and it would definitely work if it could also read the

10
00:00:34,747 --> 00:00:39,432
archive folder, so that access gets granted "just to try it", and by Friday the

11
00:00:39,432 --> 00:00:42,634
agent can reach four things nobody sat down and chose.

12
00:00:42,634 --> 00:00:45,303
Building against a written spec inverts that.

13
00:00:45,303 --> 00:00:49,988
When the build tempts you to widen something, the document makes the widening a

14
00:00:49,988 --> 00:00:54,969
decision instead of a slide, and most of the time, reading your own ceiling out loud

15
00:00:54,969 --> 00:00:56,274
is enough to stop you.

16
00:00:56,824 --> 00:00:58,492
You saw this in Section 1.

17
00:00:58,492 --> 00:01:03,368
Read it again now with different eyes: every line in it is about to become a

18
00:01:03,368 --> 00:01:05,614
specific decision inside the build.

19
00:01:05,614 --> 00:01:07,924
Scope becomes which node reads what.

20
00:01:07,924 --> 00:01:12,030
Action level becomes which nodes this workflow does not contain.

21
00:01:12,030 --> 00:01:16,586
The ceiling becomes the reason the workflow ends at a flag, not a send.

22
00:01:16,586 --> 00:01:20,115
Two lines deserve a preview of exactly where they land.

23
00:01:20,115 --> 00:01:25,119
The "urgent means" line is not background: in two lectures' time, those words,

24
00:01:25,119 --> 00:01:29,610
almost verbatim, become the instructions pasted into the agent itself.

25
00:01:29,610 --> 00:01:34,872
You already wrote the most important part of the system prompt without knowing the

26
00:01:34,872 --> 00:01:35,192
term.

27
00:01:35,192 --> 00:01:40,389
And the trial line is a commitment device: one week, every flag, each morning, is

28
00:01:40,389 --> 00:01:45,715
written down now so that Section 7's trial has a spec to check against instead of a

29
00:01:45,715 --> 00:01:46,036
vibe.

30
00:01:46,036 --> 00:01:51,104
A build that starts this way arrives at its trial with the pass mark already on

31
00:01:51,104 --> 00:01:51,489
paper.

32
00:01:52,039 --> 00:01:54,486
Notice how short that second column is.

33
00:01:54,486 --> 00:01:58,249
A narrow job, done reliably, beats a broad one done shakily.

34
00:01:58,249 --> 00:02:03,393
Every agent in this course stays deliberately narrow, and Agent 1 is the narrowest

35
00:02:03,393 --> 00:02:06,216
of the four: it never writes anything at all.

36
00:02:06,216 --> 00:02:11,109
Both columns become physically checkable in the build, and that is the part to

37
00:02:11,109 --> 00:02:11,799
appreciate.

38
00:02:11,799 --> 00:02:16,943
"Touch any inbox but Renewals" is ruled out by one form field: the trigger will be

39
00:02:16,943 --> 00:02:22,086
pointed at one named folder, so the scope is not a policy the agent follows, it is

40
00:02:22,086 --> 00:02:24,345
the only door it was given a key to.

41
00:02:24,345 --> 00:02:29,551
"Send anything, ever" is ruled out by absence: this workflow will simply contain no

42
00:02:29,551 --> 00:02:34,130
node capable of sending, and you can audit that by looking at the canvas.

43
00:02:34,130 --> 00:02:37,643
That is the quiet advantage of building agents this way.

44
00:02:37,643 --> 00:02:42,724
The safety rules are not promises buried in instructions the model might misread.

45
00:02:42,724 --> 00:02:47,366
The important ones are wiring, and wiring does not have judgement to fail.

46
00:02:47,916 --> 00:02:53,057
Four pieces, four lectures, one working agent, shipped at the end of this section as

47
00:02:53,057 --> 00:02:56,055
C01, the first of your four importable templates.

48
00:02:56,055 --> 00:03:01,135
Each piece is one sitting, none needs the one before it fresh in your head, and the

49
00:03:01,135 --> 00:03:04,501
whole section is comfortably a weekend at an easy pace.

50
00:03:04,501 --> 00:03:08,662
A practical note on what to build against: not your live work inbox.

51
00:03:08,662 --> 00:03:13,374
The whole section was built, and filmed, against a practice inbox seeded with

52
00:03:13,374 --> 00:03:18,454
realistic mail, the same forty one messages you glimpsed in Section 0, precisely so

53
00:03:18,454 --> 00:03:19,800
mistakes cost nothing.

54
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:24,941
Set one up the same way, or use any personal account you are relaxed about, and save

55
00:03:24,941 --> 00:03:29,898
the live connection for the end of the section, where it gets its own lecture and

56
00:03:29,898 --> 00:03:30,999
its own checklist.

57
00:03:30,999 --> 00:03:32,774
Next lecture: the connection.
